{"qid":"Q2662180","names":["Qocho Kingdom"],"title":"Qocho Kingdom","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Qocho Kingdom (Kingdom of Gaochang) was an Uyghur Buddhist state centred on the Turfan oasis in the eastern Tarim Basin, established by Uyghur refugees after the collapse of the Uyghur Khaganate in 840. Under the Idiqut (sacred rulers), Qocho became a major centre of Silk Road commerce and multi-confessional culture, producing Uyghur, Sogdian, Chinese, Sanskrit, and Syriac manuscripts; at its height it maintained tributary relations with both the Tang successor states and the Kitan Liao dynasty. The kingdom submitted voluntarily to the Mongols under Barquq Art Tigin around 1209, becoming one of the first polities to do so and thereby retaining local autonomy as a Mongol vassal. It was eventually absorbed into the Chagatai Khanate in the mid-thirteenth century, with the last Idiqut expelled around 1275-1283.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Qocho Kingdom's territory is well defined geographically as the Turfan oasis and surrounding region; it is attested through an unusually rich documentary record including manuscripts excavated at Turfan (Astana graves), Chinese historical sources, and Mongol period accounts, making it among the better-documented Central Asian oasis states.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Qocho","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qocho"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Uyghur Khaganate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uyghur_Khaganate"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Qocho","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Pan Tekin","nativeName":"𐽰𐾀 𐾄𐽴𐾀𐽰𐾂","roman":"Pan Tekin","